-- ── Dev Guide › GraphQL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO components (id, name, type, data, options) VALUES

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000240', 'gql_intro', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>What is GraphQL and Why Does ManuSpine Use It?</h2><p>GraphQL is a query language for APIs. Instead of a collection of fixed REST endpoints that return predetermined shapes, GraphQL exposes a single endpoint (<code>/graphql</code>) where the client declares exactly what it needs and the server returns precisely that — no more, no less. The schema is strongly typed: every field, argument, and return value has a declared type, which makes the API self-documenting and queryable.</p><h3>Why GraphQL here, and why REST too?</h3><p>ManuSpine uses both. They serve different purposes:</p><ul><li><strong>GraphQL</strong> — structured data: fetching component trees, submitting survey answers, managing users, querying survey stats. The flexible field selection and nested type resolution suit the component tree pattern well — a query can ask for a tree with its children in one round trip without over-fetching.</li><li><strong>REST</strong> — binary and streaming operations: file uploads (<code>multipart/form-data</code> is awkward in GraphQL), CSV downloads, MinIO streaming. REST handles these cleanly with standard HTTP semantics.</li></ul><p>The rule of thumb: if the response is structured JSON, use GraphQL. If it involves a binary body, a stream, or a non-standard HTTP method, use REST.</p><h3>The playground</h3><p>Navigate to <code>http://localhost:3000/graphql</code> in a browser while the stack is running. You get an interactive schema explorer where you can browse every available query and mutation, see their argument types and return shapes, and run live requests. This is the fastest way to understand what the API offers and to test a new resolver before wiring it into the frontend.</p><h3>GraphQL vs REST in practice</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>// GraphQL — one request, exactly the fields needed\nquery {\n  surveyList {\n    id\n    title\n    component_id\n  }\n}\n\n// Equivalent REST would be GET /api/surveys — returns the full row\n// including columns the frontend doesn''t need (created_at, is_active, ...)</code></pre><h3>The GraphQLJSON scalar</h3><p>The JSONB columns on <code>components</code> and <code>survey_components</code> hold arbitrary structured data (form configuration, content, options). Mapping these to hand-typed GraphQL types would be brittle — the shape changes per component type. Instead, the custom <code>GraphQLJSON</code> scalar in <code>nodejs/schema/types.js</code> passes the JSONB value through as a raw JavaScript object, both on the way out (queries) and on the way in (mutations). This is the right trade-off here: strong typing for the outer envelope, flexible typing for the JSONB interior.</p><h3>How it is assembled</h3><p>ManuSpine does not use Apollo Server. It uses <code>graphql-http</code> — a spec-compliant, zero-magic GraphQL-over-HTTP handler. The schema is built by hand in <code>nodejs/schema/index.js</code> by spreading resolver modules into a single <code>Query</code> type and a single <code>Mutation</code> type. Permissions are enforced by wrapping the <code>execute</code> function rather than inside each resolver. The following cards explain each part of this in detail.</p>"
  }', '{}'),

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000241', 'gql_schema_assembly', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>Schema Assembly in schema/index.js</h2><p>Unlike Apollo Server, ManuSpine uses <code>graphql-http</code> — a spec-compliant, minimal GraphQL-over-HTTP handler with no magic. The schema is assembled manually in <code>nodejs/schema/index.js</code> by spreading resolver modules into <code>Query</code> and <code>Mutation</code> objects.</p><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>// schema/index.js (simplified)\nconst { GraphQLSchema, GraphQLObjectType } = require(''graphql'');\nconst { createHandler } = require(''graphql-http/lib/use/express'');\n\n// [FRAMEWORK]\nconst componentResolvers = require(''./resolvers/framework/components'');\nconst surveyResolvers    = require(''./resolvers/framework/survey'');\n\n// [MY DOMAIN]\nconst myappResolvers = require(''./resolvers/myapp/things'');\n\nconst Query = new GraphQLObjectType({\n  name: ''Query'',\n  fields: {\n    ...componentResolvers.queries,\n    ...surveyResolvers.queries,\n    ...myappResolvers.queries,   // all query fields are merged here\n  },\n});\n\nconst Mutation = new GraphQLObjectType({\n  name: ''Mutation'',\n  fields: {\n    ...componentResolvers.mutations,\n    ...myappResolvers.mutations,\n  },\n});\n\nconst schema = new GraphQLSchema({ query: Query, mutation: Mutation });\n\nconst handler = createHandler({\n  schema,\n  context: (req) =&gt; ({ user: req.raw?.user ?? null }),\n  execute: permissionCheckWrapper,  // described on the Permissions card\n});\n\nmodule.exports = { schema, handler };</code></pre><p>The <code>context</code> function runs on every request and returns the context object available to all resolvers. Here it extracts <code>req.user</code> — which was attached by the JWT middleware — and passes it as <code>{ user }</code>. Resolvers receive this as their third argument.</p>"
  }', '{}'),

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000242', 'gql_resolver_structure', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>Resolver Structure</h2><p>Each domain resolver file lives at <code>nodejs/schema/resolvers/&lt;domain&gt;/things.js</code> and exports two objects: <code>queries</code> and <code>mutations</code>. Each key in those objects is one GraphQL field.</p><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>// schema/resolvers/myapp/things.js\nconst { GraphQLList, GraphQLString, GraphQLID } = require(''graphql'');\nconst { pool } = require(''../../../db'');\nconst { ThingType, ThingInputType } = require(''../../types'');  // your types\n\nconst queries = {\n  thingList: {\n    type: new GraphQLList(ThingType),   // returns an array of Thing\n    args: { type: { type: GraphQLString } },  // optional filter arg\n    async resolve(root, { type }, { user }) {\n      //  ^^^^   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^   ^^^^^^^^\n      //  parent  gql args        context\n      const sql = type\n        ? ''SELECT * FROM my_things WHERE type = $1 ORDER BY created_at DESC''\n        : ''SELECT * FROM my_things ORDER BY created_at DESC'';\n      const { rows } = await pool.query(sql, type ? [type] : []);\n      return rows;\n    },\n  },\n};\n\nconst mutations = {\n  createThing: {\n    type: ThingType,\n    args: { input: { type: ThingInputType } },\n    async resolve(root, { input }, { user }) {\n      const { rows } = await pool.query(\n        ''INSERT INTO my_things (name, data) VALUES ($1, $2::jsonb) RETURNING *'',\n        [input.name, JSON.stringify(input.data ?? {})]\n      );\n      return rows[0];\n    },\n  },\n};\n\nmodule.exports = { queries, mutations };</code></pre><p>The <code>resolve</code> function signature is always <code>(root, args, context)</code>. For top-level queries and mutations, <code>root</code> is always <code>null</code> — it is only meaningful for nested type resolvers (e.g. resolving the <code>children</code> field of a <code>Component</code> type). <code>args</code> contains the GraphQL arguments declared in the field definition. <code>context</code> contains <code>{ user }</code> from the handler''s context function.</p>"
  }', '{}'),

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000243', 'gql_permissions', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>Permission System</h2><p>GraphQL resolvers do not check auth themselves (they only owner-scope rows). Instead, <code>schema/index.js</code> wraps the <code>execute</code> function with a permission gate that runs before any resolver is called. The rules are declared in <code>permissions.js</code> as tier lists, applied <strong>identically to queries and mutations</strong>:</p><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>// permissions.js\nmodule.exports = {\n  public: [       // no token at all — componentByName only, forever\n    ''componentByName'',\n  ],\n  registered: [   // any valid JWT (any tier)\n    ''me'', ''surveyList'', ''submitAnswer'', /* ... */\n  ],\n  user: [         // tier ''user'' or ''admin'' — forks add owner-scoped ops\n  ],\n  // everything else → tier ''admin'' required (enforced by fallback)\n};\n</code></pre><p>Checks compare the JWT''s <code>tier</code> claim (''registered'' &lt; ''user'' &lt; ''admin''), never the role name — roles live in the <code>roles</code> table and each aliases onto a tier, resolved at login.</p><p>A GraphQL document executes <strong>all</strong> of its top-level selections, so the gate resolves the operation actually being executed with <code>getOperationAST</code>, collects <strong>every</strong> top-level field name (expanding fragment spreads), and fails closed on anything it cannot resolve. Checking only the first selection would let a privileged field execute unchecked, batched behind an allowed one or hidden behind a leading fragment definition.</p><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>execute: async (args) =&gt; {\n  const { document, operationName, contextValue } = args;\n  const user  = contextValue?.user;\n  const names = topLevelFieldNames(document, operationName);\n  //    ^--- getOperationAST + fragment expansion; null → fail closed\n  if (!names || names.size === 0) {\n    return { errors: [{ message: ''Unable to resolve operation'' }] };\n  }\n  for (const name of names) {   // EVERY top-level field must pass\n    if (permissions.public.includes(name)) continue;\n    if (!user) return { errors: [{ message: ''Authentication required'' }] };\n    if (permissions.user.includes(name)) {\n      if (user.tier !== ''user'' &amp;&amp; user.tier !== ''admin'') {\n        return { errors: [{ message: ''User access required'' }] };\n      }\n    } else if (!permissions.registered.includes(name)) {\n      if (user.tier !== ''admin'') {\n        return { errors: [{ message: ''Admin access required'' }] };\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return graphqlExecute(args);  // all fields passed — run the resolvers\n},</code></pre><p>Everything not listed is <strong>admin-only by default</strong> — queries and mutations alike. Adding an operation is not finished until its name is placed in the right tier in <code>permissions.js</code>. Anything in the <code>registered</code>/<code>user</code> tiers must still owner-scope inside the resolver — the tier gate protects against cross-role access, not cross-user access.</p>"
  }', '{}'),

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000244', 'gql_types', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>Defining GraphQL Types</h2><p>GraphQL types live in <code>nodejs/schema/types.js</code> (framework types) or alongside their resolver file. A type defines the shape of data a resolver returns.</p><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>const {\n  GraphQLObjectType, GraphQLInputObjectType,\n  GraphQLString, GraphQLInt, GraphQLID,\n  GraphQLBoolean, GraphQLList, GraphQLNonNull,\n} = require(''graphql'');\nconst { GraphQLJSON } = require(''../types'');  // custom scalar for JSONB\n\n// Output type — returned by queries and mutations\nconst ThingType = new GraphQLObjectType({\n  name: ''Thing'',\n  fields: () =&gt; ({                    // thunk avoids circular reference issues\n    id:         { type: GraphQLID },           // nullable by default\n    name:       { type: new GraphQLNonNull(GraphQLString) }, // ! in schema\n    data:       { type: GraphQLJSON },         // arbitrary JSONB\n    created_at: { type: GraphQLString },\n  }),\n});\n\n// Input type — used in mutation args\nconst ThingInputType = new GraphQLInputObjectType({\n  name: ''ThingInput'',\n  fields: {\n    name: { type: new GraphQLNonNull(GraphQLString) },\n    data: { type: GraphQLJSON },\n  },\n});\n\nmodule.exports = { ThingType, ThingInputType };</code></pre><p><strong>GraphQLNonNull</strong> marks a field as non-nullable (the <code>!</code> in GraphQL SDL). If a resolver returns <code>null</code> for a non-null field the entire query fails. Use it only for fields you can guarantee are always present.</p><p><strong>GraphQLJSON</strong> is a custom scalar defined in <code>schema/types.js</code> that serializes and deserializes arbitrary JavaScript objects. It maps directly to PostgreSQL JSONB columns, letting you return and accept JSONB values without manually converting to strings.</p><p><strong>Fields as a thunk (<code>() =&gt; ({...})</code>)</strong> — wrapping fields in a function defers evaluation. This allows two types to reference each other (circular references) without causing ''Cannot read properties of undefined'' errors at module load time.</p>"
  }', '{}')

ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

