-- ── Dev Guide › Python — vitals example cards ─────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO components (id, name, type, data, options) VALUES

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000252', 'py_vitals_pipeline', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>Example: Survey Analytics Pipeline</h2><p>The Patient Registration survey with its Vital Signs section wires together all three backend services to compute live analytics from survey data. This card traces one stats request from the browser to pandas and back across five stages.</p><h3>Stage 1 — Data model</h3><p>The Patient Registration survey has a Vital Signs sub-section: a nested <code>survey</code> node containing six <code>number</code> questions. Each submitted response is stored in <code>survey_answers.answers</code> as a JSONB object keyed by question UUID. The values are raw numbers and the keys are the UUIDs from <code>survey_components</code> — so renaming a question label never breaks existing answers.</p><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>-- survey_answers.answers for one submitted row (vitals portion)\n{\n  \"c51c1e5f-...-e014\": 97,    -- SpO2 (%)\n  \"c51c1e5f-...-e015\": 72,    -- Heart Rate (bpm)\n  \"c51c1e5f-...-e016\": 118,   -- Systolic BP (mmHg)\n  \"c51c1e5f-...-e017\": 76,    -- Diastolic BP (mmHg)\n  \"c51c1e5f-...-e018\": 36.8,  -- Temperature (°C)\n  \"c51c1e5f-...-e019\": 15     -- Respiratory Rate\n}</code></pre><h3>Stage 2 — Frontend calls the GraphQL resolver</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>// pwa/src/services/Api.ts\nconst stats = await ApiService.query(\n  `query { surveyStats(survey_id: \"${survey.id}\") }`\n);</code></pre><p><code>surveyStats</code> is admin-only (listed in <code>permissions.js</code>). It returns the <code>GraphQLJSON</code> scalar — an arbitrary JSON blob — so the Python response shape flows through to the frontend unchanged without being typed at the GraphQL schema layer.</p><h3>Stage 3 — Node.js flattens the question tree with a recursive CTE</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>-- schema/resolvers/framework/survey.js\nWITH RECURSIVE tree AS (\n  SELECT sc.id, sc.type, sc.data\n  FROM surveys s JOIN survey_components sc ON sc.id = s.component_id\n  WHERE s.id = $1::uuid\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT sc.id, sc.type, sc.data\n  FROM survey_components sc\n  JOIN survey_components_relationships scr ON scr.child_id = sc.id\n  JOIN tree ON tree.id = scr.parent_id\n)\nSELECT id, type, data FROM tree\nWHERE type NOT IN (''survey'', ''option'')</code></pre><p>The CTE walks the full tree regardless of nesting depth. Section containers (<code>survey</code>) and select-child nodes (<code>option</code>) are excluded — only answer-producing leaf nodes are returned. The output is a flat list: <code>[{ id, type, text }, ...]</code>.</p><h3>Stage 4 — Node.js assembles the payload and calls Python</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>const questions = qRes.rows.map(r =&gt; ({\n  id:   r.id,\n  type: r.type,\n  text: r.data?.text ?? r.type,  // human-readable label from data JSONB\n}));\nconst answers = aRes.rows.map(r =&gt; r.answers);  // raw JSONB from DB\n\nconst { data } = await axios.post(\n  `http://${process.env.PYTHON_HOST}:${process.env.PYTHON_PORT}/compute/survey-stats`,\n  { questions, answers }\n);\nreturn data;  // forwarded verbatim as the GraphQL field value</code></pre><p>Python receives a self-contained payload: a flat question list (id, type, label) and the raw answer rows. It has no knowledge of the DB schema — Node.js assembled everything. Python owns all computation; Node.js owns all data access.</p><h3>Stage 5 — Response flows back</h3><p>Python returns a JSON object with <code>engine</code>, <code>total_responses</code>, and a <code>columns</code> array (one entry per question). Node.js forwards it unchanged as the GraphQL field value. The frontend iterates <code>columns</code> and renders one stats card per numeric question — no further transformation needed.</p>"
  }', '{}'),

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000253', 'py_vitals_analytics', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>pandas Analytics — DataFrame, describe(), IQR, and Clinical Ranges</h2><p>The analytics engine lives in <code>python/api/domains/compute/routes.py</code>. It handles three question types: numeric (full statistical analysis), boolean/check (true/false counts), and categorical (value-frequency table).</p><h3>Building the DataFrame</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>def _numeric_df(questions, answers):\n    num_qs = [q for q in questions if q.type == \"number\"]\n    rows = []\n    for ans in answers:\n        row = {}\n        for q in num_qs:\n            raw = ans.get(q.id)         # look up by question UUID\n            if raw is not None:\n                row[q.text] = float(raw)  # column name = question label\n        rows.append(row)\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)</code></pre><p>Each column is named by question text (e.g. <code>SpO2 (%)</code>), not by UUID. This lets <code>CLINICAL_RANGES</code> look up reference ranges by the same human-readable key, and makes the DataFrame inspectable without a UUID lookup table.</p><h3>Descriptive statistics</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>desc = s.describe(percentiles=[0.25, 0.5, 0.75])\n# Produces: count, mean, std, min, 25%, 50%, 75%, max\nmean = round(float(desc[\"mean\"]), 2)\nstd  = round(float(desc[\"std\"]),  2) if len(s) &gt; 1 else 0.0</code></pre><h3>IQR outlier detection (Tukey fence)</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>q1, q3 = s.quantile(0.25), s.quantile(0.75)\niqr = q3 - q1\nfence_lo = q1 - 1.5 * iqr\nfence_hi = q3 + 1.5 * iqr\noutliers = sorted(s[(s &lt; fence_lo) | (s &gt; fence_hi)].tolist())</code></pre><p>Values more than 1.5 IQR below Q1 or above Q3 are statistically unusual — they are not necessarily clinically abnormal. Outliers are returned as a list of raw values so the frontend can display them as individual warning chips.</p><h3>Clinical range check</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>CLINICAL_RANGES = {\n    \"SpO2 (%)\": (95.0, 100.0),\n    \"Heart Rate (bpm)\": (60.0, 100.0),\n    \"Systolic BP (mmHg)\": (90.0, 140.0),\n    \"Diastolic BP (mmHg)\": (60.0, 90.0),\n    \"Temperature (°C)\": (36.1, 37.5),\n    \"Respiratory Rate (breaths/min)\": (12.0, 20.0),\n}\n\nclinical = CLINICAL_RANGES.get(col_name)  # keyed by question text\nif clinical:\n    lo, hi = clinical\n    abnormal_count = int(s[(s &lt; lo) | (s &gt; hi)].count())</code></pre><p>The dict key must match exactly the <code>data-&gt;&gt;''text''</code> value stored in <code>survey_components</code>. If a question label is renamed in the DB, its clinical range check silently stops applying — this is intentional: the range is anchored to meaning, not to a UUID. The frontend renders a red card border when <code>abnormal_count &gt; 0</code>.</p><h3>Categorical and boolean questions</h3><p>Questions of type <code>check</code> get a true/false count and percentage. Questions of type <code>select</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>date</code>, or <code>textarea</code> get a frequency table sorted by count descending. All non-numeric types return <code>clinical_range: null</code>, <code>abnormal_count: 0</code>, and <code>outliers: []</code> so the frontend can iterate all columns with the same code path.</p>"
  }', '{}'),

  ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000254', 'py_vitals_csv', 'contentHtml', '{
    "html": "<h2>CSV Export — StreamingResponse and Binary Forwarding</h2><p>Survey answers can be downloaded as a flat CSV without touching the stats endpoint. The export flows through two services: Node.js fetches the data, Python generates the file, Node.js streams the raw bytes to the browser.</p><h3>Python export endpoint</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>@router.post(\"/compute/survey-stats/export\")\ndef export_csv(req: SurveyStatsRequest):\n    col_names  = [q.text for q in req.questions]   # preserve order\n    id_to_text = {q.id: q.text for q in req.questions}\n\n    rows = [\n        {id_to_text[qid]: val for qid, val in ans.items() if qid in id_to_text}\n        for ans in req.answers\n    ]\n    df = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=col_names)\n    # columns= preserves order and fills gaps with NaN for unanswered questions\n\n    buf = io.StringIO()\n    df.to_csv(buf, index=False)\n    buf.seek(0)\n\n    return StreamingResponse(\n        io.BytesIO(buf.read().encode(\"utf-8\")),\n        media_type=\"text/csv\",\n        headers={\"Content-Disposition\": ''attachment; filename=\"survey_export.csv\"''},\n    )</code></pre><p>pandas handles all type coercion and CSV formatting. Rows with missing values for some questions produce <code>NaN</code> cells rather than failing. <code>StreamingResponse</code> sends the encoded bytes without buffering the entire response in FastAPI''s memory — important for large datasets.</p><h3>Node.js streaming route</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>// GET /api/surveys/:id/stats/export\n// nodejs/routes/framework/compute.js\nrouter.get(''/surveys/:id/stats/export'', async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const [qRes, aRes] = await Promise.all([\n    pool.query(SURVEY_QUESTIONS_CTE, [id]),\n    pool.query(''SELECT answers FROM survey_answers WHERE survey_id = $1::uuid'', [id]),\n  ]);\n\n  const { data, headers } = await axios.post(\n    pythonUrl, { questions, answers },\n    { responseType: ''arraybuffer'' }  // receive raw bytes, not parsed JSON\n  );\n\n  res.setHeader(''Content-Type'', ''text/csv'');\n  res.setHeader(''Content-Disposition'', headers[''content-disposition'']);\n  res.send(data);  // forward bytes unchanged — no deserialisation\n});</code></pre><p><code>responseType: ''arraybuffer''</code> tells Axios to receive the Python response as a raw byte buffer rather than attempting JSON parsing. The bytes are forwarded unchanged — Node.js is a transparent pipe for the binary content.</p><h3>Frontend download trigger</h3><pre style=\"background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);padding:14px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:0.85em;line-height:1.7\"><code>const resp = await axios.get(\n  `${API_BASE}${ENDPOINT.SURVEY_EXPORT}/${id}/stats/export`,\n  { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }, responseType: ''blob'' }\n);\nconst url = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([resp.data], { type: ''text/csv'' }));\nconst a   = document.createElement(''a'');\na.href     = url;\na.download = ''survey_export.csv'';\na.click();\nURL.revokeObjectURL(url);</code></pre><p>The browser receives a <code>Blob</code> and a temporary object URL is created for it, which triggers a file download when a synthetic <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> click is dispatched. <code>revokeObjectURL</code> immediately after the click releases the memory. No server-side temp file is ever written — the CSV exists only in memory across the three services during the request.</p>"
  }', '{}')

ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

